<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Seal backstop for every gameweek deadline. Checks every ten minutes; acts only
  inside a seal band (deadline minus 4h to deadline minus 30m).

  Install:
      cp scripts/com.pl-prediction.seal-backstop.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.pl-prediction.seal-backstop.plist

  Check it is registered:
      launchctl list | grep seal-backstop

  See what it would do right now, without dispatching: run
      scripts/seal_backstop.py with its dry-run flag (see its docstring).
  The flag is not spelled out because strict XML forbids a double hyphen inside
  a comment. launchd's parser tolerates it; Python's does not, and
  test_seal_backstop.py reads this file to check the times below.

  Remove:
      launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.pl-prediction.seal-backstop.plist

  ## Why

  GitHub delivered six to eight of the agent's twenty-four hourly ticks a day
  (measured 2026-09-24), leaving a 3.5-hour seal band empty about one day in four,
  and a missed seal can never be recovered. The workflow now ticks every fifteen
  minutes; this is the part that does not depend on GitHub's scheduler. It asks
  for one `workflow_dispatch` — which is not dropped the way schedules are — and
  only when the band is open, GW is unsealed and no agent run is in flight. See
  the docstring of scripts/seal_backstop.py for why a redundant dispatch is safe.

  ## Scope

  Every deadline, all season. It runs every ten minutes, and outside a seal band a
  check is a read of the cached FPL calendar and nothing else: no token, no
  GitHub, no log line. Inside a band (3.5 hours, so about 21 checks) it dispatches
  at most once per ten minutes, and only while the gameweek is unsealed and no
  agent run is in flight. Until 2026-09-25 it was scheduled for 10 October only.

  ## What it cannot do

  A LaunchAgent needs the Mac awake and the user logged in. A missed calendar
  run fires on wake; one that wakes after 09:30 UTC finds the band closed and does
  nothing. It is a primary defence, not a second one: the fifteen-minute cron
  was measured not to raise GitHub's delivery (6.6 runs a day for a */15
  workflow, 6.5 for an hourly one), while a dispatch started in 5 seconds.
  Its twin on Vercel's cron, frontend/app/api/cron/seal-backstop, covers the
  hours the Mac is asleep once GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN is set there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.pl-prediction.seal-backstop</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/python3</string>
        <string>/Users/tusk-jvb/dev/pl-prediction-engine/scripts/seal_backstop.py</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Every ten minutes while the Mac is awake. launchd runs a missed interval
         once on wake, never a backlog. Ten, not fifteen: RECENT_RUN in the script
         is ten minutes, so a dispatched run is left alone until it could have
         finished, and the band still gets about twenty-one checks. -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>600</integer>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/tusk-jvb/dev/pl-prediction-engine</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/tusk-jvb/Library/Logs/pl-prediction-seal-backstop.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/tusk-jvb/Library/Logs/pl-prediction-seal-backstop.log</string>

    <!-- Never at load: installing it must not dispatch anything. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
